const projects = [
  {
    title: "Vertical-axis Aesthetic Wind Turbine",
    org: "Virginia Tech - Team 9 (Miguel Pinho, Dia Sashittal, Nikhil Pimpalapure, Aiden Lowery, Heidi Hort-Ly, Aiden Okabayashi)",
    role: "My role: Prototyping & CAD",
    tags: ["SolidWorks", "CFD Simulation", "Additive Manufacturing", "Python", "Renewable Energy"],
    summary: [
      "An aesthetically pleasing vertical-axis wind turbine designed to sit on top of a Virginia Tech dorm and solve the problem of backup power - generating and storing long-term energy so critical systems like AC, heat, and lighting stay running during an outage.",
      "The team moved forward with my hybrid Savonius-Darrieus model because it captures wind from any direction, so it keeps generating without needing to be pointed into the wind. It also scored highest in nearly every criterion of our decision matrix, largely because the Savonius central rotor paired with Darrieus blades produces high torque at low wind speeds - exactly what a variable-wind urban campus needs.",
      "On the prototyping and CAD side I focused on integrating the two turbine types structurally and functionally: refining the Darrieus components for low drag and high strength, engineering a clean mating surface between the vertical blades and the center hub so it survives centrifugal loading at speed, and using additive manufacturing to keep the Savonius rotor perfectly centered and balanced on the drive shaft. That meant learning advanced SolidWorks mates, lofted boss/base and fillet work, plus Design for Additive Manufacturing tolerancing and shelling so 3D-printed parts fit on the first assembly.",
      "Stability is the tradeoff we accepted for efficiency and effectiveness, and it is the next thing we plan to solve."
    ],
